WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.100
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.670
This piece of work is an amazing application
of deep neural networks, that performs semantic

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:12.920
scene completion from only one depth image.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.800
This depth image is the colorful image that
you see here, where the colors denote how

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.320
far away different objects are from our camera.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:26.509
We can create these images inexpensively with
commodity hardware, for instance, Microsoft's

00:00:26.509 --> 00:00:30.210
Kinect has a depth sensor sensor that is suitable
for this task.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:34.840
The scene completion part means that from
this highly incomplete depth information,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.220
the algorithm reconstructs the geometry for
the entirety of the room.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.480
Even parts, that are completely missing from
our images or things that are occluded!

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.860
The output is what computer graphics researchers
like to call a volumetric representation or

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:55.180
a voxel array, which is essentially a large
collection of tiny Lego pieces that build

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.180
up the scene.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:01.290
But this is not all because the semantic part
means that the algorithm actually understands

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:06.170
what we're looking at, and thus, is able to
classify different parts of the scene.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:12.079
These classes include walls, windows, floors,
sofas, and other furniture.

00:01:12.079 --> 00:01:16.759
Previous works were able to do scene completion
and geometry classification, but the coolest

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:21.810
part of this algorithm is that it not only
does these steps way better, but it does them

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.390
both at the very same time.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.560
This work uses a 3D convolutional neural network
to accomplish this task.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:35.360
The 3D part is required for this learning algorithm to be able to operate on this kind of volumetric data.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.080
As you can see, the results are excellent, and are remarkably close to the ground truth data.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.560
If you remember, not so long ago, I flipped
out when I've seen the first neural network-based

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.940
techniques that understood 3D geometry from
2D images.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:56.569
That technique used a much more complicated
architecture, a generative adversarial network,

00:01:56.569 --> 00:02:01.210
which also didn't do scene completion and
on top of that, the resolution of the output

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:07.009
was way lower, which intuitively means that
Lego pieces were much larger.

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:08.580
This is insanity.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:14.890
The rate of progress in machine learning research
is just stunning, probably even for you seasoned

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.300
Fellow Scholars who watch Two Minute Papers
and have high expectations.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:24.230
We've had plenty of previous episodes about
the inner workings of different kinds of neural

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:25.230
networks.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.390
I've put some links to them in the video description,
make sure to have a look if you wish to brush

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.070
up on your machine learning kung fu a bit.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:37.500
The authors also published a new dataset to
solve these kind of problems in future research

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.670
works, and, it is also super useful because
the output of their technique can be compared

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.470
to ground truth data.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.390
When new solutions pop up in the future, this
dataset can be used as a yardstick to compare

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:50.510
results with.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:53.470
The source code for this project is also available.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.010
Tinkerers rejoice!

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.560
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

